ss the four volts so that's  four volts up here  but what on earth you know is down here  it says 100 nano volts  i mean that's a very hard concept to  wrap your mind around  it's what's a nano volt and what we can  do with a nano volt  in general when we're analyzing and  measuring things and  this class of devices that i'm measuring  we're not interested in actual voltages  we're interested in ratios we want to  know if our signals up here and our  distortions  down here what is that distance between  the  two of them because then we can use  psychoacoustic research  in telling us how audible that amplitude  is relative to our main signal so we're  always interested in the ratio of these  things  not as an absolute voltage i mean  there's  you know if i raise both of them high  the ratio stays constant that's what  determines audibility  so here we don't have that uh we don't  know you know we could try to do the  math in our head  that's very difficult so what we can do  is convert this thing to  instead of volts we can convert it to  decibels  relative to volts it says dbv hopefully  you can read that  and this is basically saying everything  i'm going to show you on this graph now  is  relative as a ratio to 1 volt  and so  it's showing us a graph now that it's  the numbers now for one thing  look a lot more uh sane now  um and uh  because they're just normal numbers we  don't have nanovolts and and what have  you  and uh we see there's a minus 140 down  here  and so if we look at this spike we might  be tempted to think that this is  at -140 or thereabouts uh turns out  that's wrong  why is that wrong because if we look up  here  the peak of our signal is not at zero  it's actually at 11 or something like  that and  if that's the case then this is not 140  db below that it's uh  you got to take that 11 and subtract it  from this  so it's a very inconvenient unit to use  this dbv  there's a close cousin of it called dbu  it's just another ratio and it's got the  same problem  but yet you see it used a lot in a lot  of measurements  that people show it's just a super  confusing thing  again you can go google for these things  and learn what dbu and dbv are  mathematically just simple formula but  it's just very hard to wrap your head  around it even i can't keep it straight  and what i do is that i avoid both of  these things  i don't care how many volts we really  have as i explained we want to know the  ratio  so there's a clever thing the analyzer  can do is  where i can try to take an  arbitrary voltage and make that the zero  manually and then we can see all the  distortion and noise then  fall under that now i've set it right  now to dbr a which is  db reference an a it's got two of them a  and b  but it's not programmed correctly  because it's still the peak of this  output is above zero but a clever thing  i can do is go over here in references  and i can set that reference to what is  being measured which is this 3.99 volts  right now oh sorry you don't see the  pop-ups over here so  just take my word for it that i'm  setting the dpr reference to uh  four volts now that i said that my  reference to be this  the source signal the wanted signal now  when we look down here we can see  exactly where the uh  uh these peaks are the numbers here now  100 correspond to the uh values that we  want to know from psychoacoustics point  of view  so this signal we can say is minus 148  db  this means um the  peak that you see at 60 hertz and  there's a little distortion over here  minus 150 db i can actually  mess this up by the generator also has a  dac  and instead of using the uh analog  generator  you can use a dac so let me go ahead and  reset this so  now the the digital generator that it  has is generating signal  and you can see it has far more  distortion  like most stacks would and here now we  can read these  so nicely now we can see that this third  harmonic is at minus 115 db  and we look at down here our sine add is  -112  so that's actually pretty close it's  saying that this is basically the  problem area add a little bit of noise  to it which is what this number is  sine hat is sum of noise and distortion  and we get to  1 12. and you see the power of  of using a a log scale or ratio  and also having it set to zero  when this is not set to zero it just  causes nothing but confusion so  my advice so anybody that publishes  these measurements is please please  don't use dba  dbv or dbu  always use db relative and then set that  to be  zero once we have a zero we said we  immediately know how low this amplitude  is  i've explained that when these  distortions are around  -115 means they're absolutely inaudible  and we can see that's the case even  though this is the dirty  generator in my audio precision it still  is  this noise is below audibility  uh so hopefully it's concepts understood  again you can go  google for it online and learn more but  what we're trying to do is have a scale  that's easy to understand  and also be able to cover very high  range of numbers that's what a log  scale is let's do and db is a log scale  in that it allows us to go way down deep  to very very small numbers  yet deal with you know small values like  minus 150 rather than nano volts and  pico volts and  you go down way way way down to numbers  that you can't just wrap your head  around  um i've also mentioned this in the past  that we can take any ratio a  phd plus n which is harmonic distortion  and noise is a ratio  right it's a percentage it says what is  if we add all these together on the  noise what is the ratio to this  you can express that as a percentage but  as you see here is very hard to remember  this number  uh zero zero zero zero two five zero  whatever it is whereas you know  one twelve all right great i can  remember one twelfth that's a lot easier  to remember  and uh let me leave you with a hint uh  before i finish even though we say these  things are decibels don't ever call them  decibels  uh always call them a db now  if you look around you'll see that the  two are equivalent the units we write as  db but this stands for decibel  when you say decibel it  can't imply that you don't know what  you're talking about whereas when you  say  dv sounds like you know a lot more than  you do  so whenever you're walking around and uh  talking to somebody else technical just  throw the word db around  and that person may think that you know  a lot more than you do  but you said decibel is not something we  as engineers usually  spell out so you want to look cool  use db okay see you in the next review  next uh video bye bye

